## 3. EMACPS register / descriptor definitions and pre-implemented helpers

### 3a. Frame + ring types
`drivers/emacps/xemacps_example.h:92-98`:
```c
#define ROUND(a, n)     (((((uint64_t)(a))+(n)-1)) & ~((n)-1))
typedef char EthernetFrame[ROUND(XEMACPS_MAX_FRAME_SIZE,64)] __attribute__ ((aligned(64)));
#define RXBD_CNT 32 /* Number of RxBDs to use */
#define TXBD_CNT 32 /* Number of TxBDs to use */
```
`drivers/emacps/xemacps.h:472-482`: `XEMACPS_MTU 1500U`, `XEMACPS_HDR_SIZE 14U`, `XEMACPS_TRL_SIZE 4U`, `XEMACPS_MAX_FRAME_SIZE = 1518`. So the **kernel** `EthernetFrame` is **1536 bytes** (1518 rounded to 64), 64-byte aligned.
The **user-side** `EthernetFrame` is different: `tiny_libc/include/os.h:41-42` → `typedef char EthernetFrame[XEMACPS_MAX_FRAME_SIZE] __attribute__ ((aligned(64)));` = **1518 bytes**. Tests size their buffers with the 1518 version; the kernel copies only `rx_len[i]` bytes contiguously, so this mismatch is benign.

BD type: `XEmacPs_Bd` (see `xemacps_bd.h`), alignment `XEMACPS_BD_ALIGNMENT 64U` (`xemacps_hw.h:84`).

### 3b. Register offsets (`drivers/emacps/xemacps_hw.h:128-308`), the ones that matter
```
XEMACPS_NWCTRL_OFFSET   0x00  XEMACPS_NWCFG_OFFSET  0x04  XEMACPS_NWSR_OFFSET 0x08
XEMACPS_DMACR_OFFSET    0x10  XEMACPS_TXSR_OFFSET   0x14
XEMACPS_RXQBASE_OFFSET  0x18  XEMACPS_TXQBASE_OFFSET 0x1C  XEMACPS_RXSR_OFFSET 0x20
XEMACPS_ISR_OFFSET      0x24  XEMACPS_IER_OFFSET    0x28
XEMACPS_IDR_OFFSET      0x2C  XEMACPS_IMR_OFFSET    0x30
XEMACPS_PHYMNTNC_OFFSET 0x34
```
Direction ids (`xemacps_hw.h:98-99`): `XEMACPS_SEND 1U`, `XEMACPS_RECV 2U`.

Key masks:
- NWCTRL (`:315-333`): `FLUSH_DPRAM 0x00040000`, `STARTTX 0x00000200`, `MDEN 0x10`, `TXEN 0x08`, `RXEN 0x04`, `LOOPEN 0x02`.
- TXSR (`:422-437`): `HRESPNOK 0x100`, `URUN 0x40`, `TXCOMPL 0x20`, `BUFEXH 0x10`, `TXGO 0x08`, `RXOVR 0x04`, `FRAMERX 0x02`, `USEDREAD 0x01`.
- RXSR (`:444-451`): `HRESPNOK 0x08`, `RXOVR 0x04`, `FRAMERX 0x02`, `BUFFNA 0x01`.
- Interrupt bits, same layout in ISR/IER/IDR/IMR (`:486-506`): `HRESPNOK 0x800`, `RXOVR 0x400`, `TXCOMPL 0x80`, `TXEXH 0x40`, `RETRY 0x20`, `URUN 0x10`, `TXUSED 0x08`, `RXUSED 0x04`, `FRAMERX 0x02`, `MGMNT 0x01`, `XEMACPS_IXR_ALL_MASK 0x00007FFF`;
  `XEMACPS_IXR_TX_ERR_MASK = TXEXH|RETRY|URUN`, `XEMACPS_IXR_RX_ERR_MASK = HRESPNOK|RXUSED|RXOVR`.

### 3c. Descriptor field layout (`xemacps_hw.h:534-594`)
```
XEMACPS_BD_ADDR_OFFSET     0x00   /* word0: buffer address (+ rx wrap/new bits) */
XEMACPS_BD_STAT_OFFSET     0x04   /* word1: status/length */
XEMACPS_BD_ADDR_HI_OFFSET  0x08
TX word1: USED 0x80000000, WRAP 0x40000000, RETRY, URUN, EXH, TCP, NOCRC 0x10000,
          LAST 0x8000, LEN_MASK 0x3FFF
RX word0: WRAP 0x00000002, NEW 0x00000001, ADD_MASK 0xFFFFFFFC
RX word1: BCAST 0x80000000, ... EOF 0x8000, SOF 0x4000, LEN_MASK 0x1FFF
```
BD accessors (`xemacps_bd.h`): `XEmacPs_BdRead/BdWrite` (:135,:154), `BdSetAddressTx` (:171/:177), `BdSetAddressRx` (:197/:204), `BdSetStatus` (:223), `BdGetStatus` (:243), `BdSetLength` (:281), `BdGetLength` (:307, masks with `XEMACPS_RXBUF_LEN_MASK`), `BdSetLast/ClearLast` (:365/:383), `BdClearRxWrap` (:400), `BdIsRxWrap` (:437), `BdClearRxNew` (:491), `BdSetRxNew` (:497), `BdIsRxNew` (:513), `BdSetTxUsed/ClearTxUsed/IsTxUsed` (:532/:551/:568), `BdClear` (:117).
`XEmacPs_BdSetRxWrap` is **not** a macro — it is a real function, `drivers/emacps/xemacps_bdring.c:1045-1057`, prototype `extern void XEmacPs_BdSetRxWrap(UINTPTR BdPtr);` at `xemacps_bd.h:417`.

### 3d. Register/interrupt macros (`drivers/emacps/xemacps.h`)
```c
#define XEmacPs_ReadReg(BaseAddress, RegOffset)  XEmacPs_In32((BaseAddress) + (u32)(RegOffset))          // hw.h:623
#define XEmacPs_WriteReg(BaseAddress, RegOffset, Data) XEmacPs_Out32(...)                                 // hw.h:644
#define XEmacPs_IntEnable(InstancePtr, Mask)  XEmacPs_WriteReg(...->Config.BaseAddress, XEMACPS_IER_OFFSET, ((Mask) & XEMACPS_IXR_ALL_MASK));   // :617
#define XEmacPs_IntDisable(InstancePtr, Mask) XEmacPs_WriteReg(..., XEMACPS_IDR_OFFSET, ((Mask) & XEMACPS_IXR_ALL_MASK));                       // :638
#define XEmacPs_Transmit(InstancePtr) ... NWCTRL |= XEMACPS_NWCTRL_STARTTX_MASK                            // :699
#define xil_printf printk                                                                                  // :847
#define MACB_ADDR_REMAP(ADDR) (((uint32_t)0x50000000) | (((uint32_t)(ADDR)) & ((uint32_t)0x0FFFFFFF)))     // :849
```
`XEmacPs_Config` (`:529-534`) = `{u16 DeviceId; UINTPTR BaseAddress; u8 IsCacheCoherent;}`; instance struct `XEmacPs` at `:542-566`.
`xil_utils.h:7-24`: `Xil_Out32`→`writel`, `Xil_In32`→`readl`, `Xil_DCacheFlushRange(addr,size)` and `Xil_DCacheInvalidateRange` both just call `local_flush_dcache_all()`.
`XEmacPs_SetQueuePtr` (`xemacps.c:446-471`) does `QPtr = kva2pa(QPtr);` then writes `MACB_ADDR_REMAP(QPtr & ULONG64_LO_MASK)` to `XEMACPS_TXQBASE_OFFSET` / `XEMACPS_RXQBASE_OFFSET`.

### 3e. Already-implemented driver entry points (`drivers/emacps/xemacps_main.c`), declared in `xemacps_example.h:118-123`
```c
LONG EmacPsInit(XEmacPs *EmacPsInstancePtr);                                            // :402
LONG EmacPsSend(XEmacPs *EmacPsInstancePtr, EthernetFrame *TxFrame, size_t length);     // :446
LONG EmacPsWaitSend(XEmacPs *EmacPsInstancePtr);                                        // :478
LONG EmacPsRecv(XEmacPs *EmacPsInstancePtr, EthernetFrame *RxFrame, int num_packet);    // :486
LONG EmacPsWaitRecv(XEmacPs *EmacPsInstancePtr, int num_packet, u32* RxFrLen);          // :540
void handle_xemacps_irq();                                                              // :840
```
Behaviour worth knowing:
- `EmacPsSend` sets `BdSetLength(BdTxPtr, length)`, `BdClearTxUsed`, flushes dcache, `XEmacPs_SetQueuePtr(..., XEMACPS_SEND)`, enables TXEN, `XEmacPs_Transmit`.
- `EmacPsWaitSend` spins on `XEmacPs_BdIsTxUsed(BdTxPtr)`.
- `EmacPsRecv` disables RXEN, clears `XEMACPS_RXSR_OFFSET` to `0xf`, resets `num_packet` RX BDs (`BdWrite(STAT,0)`, `BdClearRxNew`, `BdClearRxWrap`), sets wrap on the last one, flushes, `SetQueuePtr(..., XEMACPS_RECV)`, re-enables RXEN.
- `EmacPsWaitRecv` spins per BD on `XEmacPs_BdIsRxNew` and fills `RxFrLen[i] = XEmacPs_BdGetLength(CurPtr)`.
- `EmacPsInit` still contains an **unimplemented TODO** (`xemacps_main.c:435-441`) for enabling TX/RX interrupts — deliberately left there because `do_net_irq_mode` is the thing that enables them. The same TODO is present in the P6 tree, i.e. it stays unimplemented:
```c
    /* Enable TX and RX interrupts */
    /* TODO: 
     * NOTE: you can use XEmacPs_IntEnable and XEmacPs_IntDisable
     *       enable these bits: 
     *          (XEMACPS_IXR_TX_ERR_MASK | XEMACPS_IXR_RX_ERR_MASK |
     *          (u32)XEMACPS_IXR_FRAMERX_MASK | (u32)XEMACPS_IXR_TXCOMPL_MASK)
     */
```
- Globals defined in `xemacps_main.c`: `bd_space[0x200000]` (:178), `RxBdSpacePtr = &bd_space[0]`, `TxBdSpacePtr = &bd_space[0x10000]` (:266-267), `XEmacPs_Bd *BdTxPtr, *BdRxPtr` (:259-260), `xemacps_config` (:201), `GemVersion`, `Platform`, `XPS_SYS_CTRL_BASEADDR` (:134).
- `xemacps_example_util.c:62-69`: `XEmacPs EmacPsInstance;`, `EmacPsMAC[] = {0x00,0x0a,0x35,0x01,0x02,0x03}`, `BroadcastMAC[] = {0xff × 6}`, `DestMAC[] = {0xec,0x8e,0xb5,0x59,0x38,0xfb}`.

### 3f. The two completion handlers already reference net.c's globals
`xemacps_main.c:878-898` (`XEmacPsSendHandler`) and `:915-951` (`XEmacPsRecvHandler`) are static and already written:
```c
static void XEmacPsSendHandler(void *Callback)
{
    ...
    do_unblock(net_send_queue.next);
    u32 Reg = XEmacPs_ReadReg(...->Config.BaseAddress, XEMACPS_ISR_OFFSET);
    Reg = Reg | XEMACPS_IXR_TXCOMPL_MASK;
    XEmacPs_WriteReg(...->Config.BaseAddress, XEMACPS_ISR_OFFSET, Reg);
    send_transaction = 0;
}
```
```c
static void XEmacPsRecvHandler(void *Callback)
{
    ...
    XEmacPs_Bd* CurPtr = BdRxPtr;
    for(int i = 0; i < _num_packet; i++){
        while(!XEmacPs_BdIsRxNew(CurPtr)) { Xil_DCacheFlushRange(0, 64); }
        rx_len[i] = XEmacPs_BdGetLength(CurPtr);
        CurPtr++;
    }
    uintptr_t rx_curr = _addr;
    for(int i = 0; i < _num_packet; i++){
        memcpy((uint8_t*)rx_curr, (uint8_t*)&rx_buffers[i], rx_len[i]);
        *(_frLength + i) = rx_len[i];
        rx_curr += rx_len[i];
    }
    do_unblock(net_recv_queue.next);
    ... ISR |= XEMACPS_IXR_FRAMERX_MASK ...
    recv_transaction = 0;
}
```
**This dictates the contract for net.c**: it must set `recv_transaction`/`send_transaction` to 1 before kicking off a transaction, must populate `_addr` (kernel VA), `_num_packet`, `_frLength` (kernel VA) before blocking, and must enqueue the blocked PCB on `net_recv_queue` / `net_send_queue` (the handler unblocks `queue.next`).

---

## 4. How a NIC interrupt reaches the driver

`init/main.c:172-205` (kernel `main`) — device discovery + PLIC bring-up:
```c
    uint32_t slcr_bade_addr = 0, ethernet_addr = 0, plic_addr = 0, nr_irqs = 0;
    slcr_bade_addr = sbi_read_fdt(SLCR_BADE_ADDR);
    ethernet_addr  = sbi_read_fdt(ETHERNET_ADDR);
    plic_addr      = sbi_read_fdt(PLIC_ADDR);
    nr_irqs        = sbi_read_fdt(NR_IRQS);
    ...
    XPS_SYS_CTRL_BASEADDR = (uintptr_t)ioremap((uint64_t)slcr_bade_addr, NORMAL_PAGE_SIZE);
    xemacps_config.BaseAddress = (uintptr_t)ioremap((uint64_t)ethernet_addr, NORMAL_PAGE_SIZE);
    xemacps_config.DeviceId        = 0;
    xemacps_config.IsCacheCoherent = 0;
    uintptr_t _plic_addr = (uintptr_t)ioremap((uint64_t)plic_addr, 0x4000 * NORMAL_PAGE_SIZE);
    plic_init(_plic_addr, nr_irqs);
    long status = EmacPsInit(&EmacPsInstance);
```
(`enum SBI_READY_FDT_TYPE { TIMEBASE, SLCR_BADE_ADDR, ETHERNET_ADDR, PLIC_ADDR, NR_IRQS }`, `arch/riscv/include/sbi.h:119-125`.)

`include/os/irq.h:34-61` — the constants:
```c
enum IrqCode { IRQC_U_SOFT=0, IRQC_S_SOFT=1, IRQC_M_SOFT=3, IRQC_U_TIMER=4,
               IRQC_S_TIMER=5, IRQC_M_TIMER=7, IRQC_U_EXT=8, IRQC_S_EXT=9,
               IRQC_M_EXT=11, IRQC_COUNT };
enum ExcCode { EXCC_INST_MISALIGNED=0, EXCC_INST_ACCESS=1, EXCC_INST_ILLEGAL=2,
               EXCC_BREAKPOINT=3, EXCC_LOAD_ACCESS=5, EXCC_STORE_ACCESS=7,
               EXCC_SYSCALL=8, EXCC_INST_PAGE_FAULT=12, EXCC_LOAD_PAGE_FAULT=13,
               EXCC_STORE_PAGE_FAULT=15, EXCC_COUNT };
```

`kernel/irq/irq.c:31` registers the external-interrupt path: `irq_table[IRQC_S_EXT] = (handler_t)plic_handle_irq;`, dispatched from `interrupt_helper` (`irq.c:47-59`) which first does `cpu_id = get_current_cpu_id(); current_cpu_running = current_running[cpu_id];`.

`drivers/plic.c:122-133`:
```c
void plic_handle_irq(regs_context_t *regs)
{
	struct plic_handler *handler = &plic_handlers;
	void *claim = handler->hart_base + CONTEXT_CLAIM;
	int hwirq;
	csr_clear(sie, SIE_SEIE);
	while ((hwirq = readl(claim))) {
			handle_irq(regs, hwirq);
	}
	csr_set(sie, SIE_SEIE);
}
```
PLIC layout constants `plic.c:32-50`: `PRIORITY_BASE 0`, `PRIORITY_PER_ID 4`, `ENABLE_BASE 0x2000`, `ENABLE_PER_HART 0x80`, `CONTEXT_BASE 0x200000`, `CONTEXT_PER_HART 0x1000`, `CONTEXT_THRESHOLD 0x00`, `CONTEXT_CLAIM 0x04`. `plic_init` (`:135-163`) sets threshold 0, enables every hwirq `1..nr_irqs`, and additionally `plic_irq_unmask(33); /* for qemu to interrupt */`.

`kernel/irq/irq.c:66-73` — **already implemented** despite the TODO comment:
```c
void handle_irq(regs_context_t *regs, int irq)
{
    // TODO: 
    // handle external irq from network device
    // let PLIC know that handle_irq has been finished
    handle_xemacps_irq();
    plic_irq_eoi(irq);
}
```

`drivers/emacps/xemacps_main.c:840-861`:
```c
void handle_xemacps_irq()
{
    int send_flag = 0, recv_flag = 0;
    Xil_DCacheFlushRange(0, 64);
    u32 reg;
    reg = XEmacPs_ReadReg(EmacPsInstance.Config.BaseAddress, XEMACPS_TXSR_OFFSET);
    if(reg & XEMACPS_TXSR_TXCOMPL_MASK) { send_flag = 1; }
    reg = XEmacPs_ReadReg(EmacPsInstance.Config.BaseAddress, XEMACPS_RXSR_OFFSET);
    if(reg & XEMACPS_RXSR_FRAMERX_MASK) { recv_flag = 1; }
    assert(send_flag || recv_flag);

    if(send_flag && send_transaction){ XEmacPsSendHandler((void*)&EmacPsInstance); }
    else if(recv_flag && recv_transaction){ XEmacPsRecvHandler((void*)&EmacPsInstance); }
}
```
Note the `assert(send_flag || recv_flag)` — if net.c leaves stale TXSR/RXSR status bits or enables interrupts with no transaction in flight, this asserts and kills the kernel. (The generic `XEmacPs_IntrHandler` in `xemacps_intr.c:144` is **not** on this path; it is compiled but unused.)

