## 4. What P5's `init/main.c` expects

**The one functional change vs P4** (`P5/init/main.c:125`):
```c
    uintptr_t entry = load_elf((unsigned char*)binary, length, (uintptr_t)pcb[0].pgdir, alloc_page_helper);
```
P4 passes `get_kva_of` here. `load_elf`'s callback type (`include/os/elf.h:140-142`, identical in both) is:
```c
static inline uintptr_t load_elf(
    unsigned char elf_binary[], unsigned length, uintptr_t pgdir,
    uintptr_t (*prepare_page_for_va)(uintptr_t va, uintptr_t pgdir));
/* prepare_page_for_kva should return a kernel virtual address */
```
So **`alloc_page_helper(va, pgdir)` must (a) accept an unaligned `va`, (b) create the mapping if absent, (c) return the KVA of the whole containing frame** — the loop does `bytes_of_page[j]` for `j` up to `NORMAL_PAGE_SIZE`, so the returned pointer must be the page base. P4's implementation already does exactly this (`va = ROUNDDOWN(va, PAGE_SIZE); ... return pa2kva(get_pa(*pte));`).

Verbatim `init_pcb()` from `P5/init/main.c:69-132`:
